      "created_at": "2017-01-30T18:30:11Z",
      "updated_at": "2017-01-30T18:30:11Z",
      "body": "@thegranddesign I was able to reproduce the error you're experiencing. \r\nGiven the following configuration, and assuming the module at the specified filepath attaches the supplied policies (Preconfigured AWS Policies) to generated iam_roles:\r\n```\r\nmodule \"api_server_instance_profile\" {\r\n  source = \"./modules/instance_profile\"\r\n\r\n  name = \"api-server\"\r\n  policy_ids = \"arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/IAMUserChangePassword,arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/service-role/AmazonEC2RoleforSSM,arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/service-role/AWSConfigRole,arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/AmazonAPIGatewa\\\r\nyAdministrator,arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/AmazonAPIGatewayInvokeFullAccess,arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/AmazonAppStreamReadOnlyAccess\"\r\n  policy_count = 6\r\n}\r\n\r\nmodule \"bastion_instance_profile\" {\r\n  source = \"./modules/instance_profile\"\r\n  name = \"bastion\"\r\n  policy_ids = \"arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/IAMUserChangePassword\"\r\n  policy_count = 1\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nI am able to continuously reproduce the error you are experiencing. However, if I change the list of policy id's passed in the second module declaration, to use policies that were not already attached in the first module's declaration, I can no longer get the issue to reproduce.\r\n\r\nEx:\r\n```\r\nmodule \"api_server_instance_profile\" {\r\n  source = \"./modules/instance_profile\"\r\n\r\n  name = \"api-server\"\r\n  policy_ids = \"arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/IAMUserChangePassword,arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/service-role/AmazonEC2RoleforSSM,arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/service-role/AWSConfigRole,arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/AmazonAPIGatewa\\\r\nyAdministrator,arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/AmazonAPIGatewayInvokeFullAccess,arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/AmazonAppStreamReadOnlyAccess\"\r\n  policy_count = 6\r\n}\r\n\r\nmodule \"bastion_instance_profile\" {\r\n  source = \"./modules/instance_profile\"\r\n  name = \"bastion\"\r\n  policy_ids = \"arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/AmazonZocaloFullAccess\"\r\n  policy_count = 1\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis is because an `iam_policy` can only have at max 1 policy attachment resource bound to it, as the `iam_policy_attachment` resource will manage every attachment on that policy. This is why we were experiencing the diff between policy attachments for each attachment resource. \r\n\r\nTo sum up, in order to fix the issue we need to ensure that each `iam_policy` has only one `iam_policy_attachment` attached to it. This may result in multiple policies that have the same content but different names, or restructuring the policy_attachment resource to maintain the 1-1 relationship between an `iam_policy` and the `iam_policy_attachment` resources.\r\n\r\nClosing as not a bug, but happy to discuss further. Thanks!",
      "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
      "pin": null,
